 { Outposts are extremely valuable for a variety of reasons, and were dealt with in a separate chapter earlier in the course. We'll revisit the topic in this section, since outposts can be a great ally in the fight for open files. Let's show what this means by way of examples. In this first position, White has just made a big mistake by exchanging knights on e4. } 1... { -KEY- } dxe4 { The d-file opens up, and at the same time Black secures an outpost on d3. } 2. Rg3 $2 { Too ambitious.There's no chance for the attack to succeed against Black's ideal pawn cover, while now the rook can play no part in defense. } ( 2. Rff1 { White needed to scramble back to f1 in order to fight for the open file, but even here Black would squeeze the opponent due to having the more active pieces: } Rd3 3. Bd4 c5 4. Bb2 Qa5 $1 { The outpost on d3 is so powerful that White just cannot shake the rook away: } 5. Rfd1 Rfd8 6. Rxd3 Rxd3 $19 { White is lost, since they are not in time to challenge the remaining rook. For example: } 7. Rd1 Qxa2 $1 8. Rxd3 exd3 9. Qxd3 Qxb2 $19 { Black is up a full piece. } ) 
g6 $1 { A nice move. Black uses one tempo to shut down any chance of a kingside attack. } 3. Rg4 Rd3 $1 { Using the outpost to invade the White camp. } 4. Re1 Rfd8 5. Bc1 { White has been forced to play one passive move after another, and now Black finishes the game by targeting White's worst placed piece, the over-ambitious rook on g4! } h5 6. Rg3 Bh4 $19 { There is nowhere to run for the rook, so Black can win some material before making further progress on the queenside & centre with a move like ...Qa5. Whenever there is a possibility that a file will get opened and there is an outpost that can be used along that file, it is crucial to stay alert. A reckless decision at this point of the game can pave the way for success or defeat. Let's take a look at a famous example of this next. } 
 *

