 { This position was reached in the game Chekhover,V - Capablanca, J (1935) . Black is in command here. Not only can he choose to open up the h-file at exactly the right moment; he will also have an outpost on h4 which he can use to funnel his pieces into the enemy camp. } 42... { -KEY- } Qg8 { Capablanca is in no rush to open up the h-file, preferring to first build up the piece pressure on the kingside. } 43. Qc1 ( { The problem for White is that they can't clear the tension themselves since after } 43. gxh5 Nxh5 $17 { Black has a power outpost to take aim at on f4, while the h3 pawn is now an easy target. } ) 
Kc7 44. Qe3 hxg4 { With all of Black's pieces on the kingside, it's now a good time to crack open the h-file. } 45. hxg4 Rgh7 $1 46. Qc1 { Lending support to the rook on h1. White is hoping that Black would just exchange everything off on h1, when the position would become drawish. Black however, had much greater ambitions. } ( 46. Rxh7 Rxh7 47. Rh1 $4 { The attempt to swap rooks loses on the spot. } Rxh1 48. Bxh1 Nxg4 $19 ) 
Rh4 $1 { Anchoring the rook on the h4 outpost, and adding to the pressure against White's kingside pawns. } 47. Rxh4 Rxh4 48. Rg2 ( 48. Rh1 $2 { fails to } Nxg4 { winning a pawn. } ) 
Qg7 ( { The slow route to the h-file is necessary, since the immediate } 48... Qh7 { would have lost the important pawn after } 49. Qxg5 { . } ) 
49. Ke2 $2 { White starts to go wrong, though by this point only computer-like precision would offer any chances of holding a draw. } Qh6 { The position is so miserable to defend, that the game is effectively over. White has a bricked in bishop on f3, while Black is spoiled for choice in terms of entry squares along the h-file. } 50. Kd3 Rh1 { Invading deeper into the position. } 51. Qd2 Qh3 52. Qe2 Rb1 $1 { Both of Black's heavy pieces are running around the White half of the board, and there is little left to do. } 53. Kc3 Re1 $19 ( { White resigned, in view of } 53... Re1 53. Qd3 Rxe4 $1 55. Bxe4 Nxe4 56. Kc2 Qxg2 { . Another reason why outposts are difficult to deal with is that if the defending side exchanges the outposted piece it can lead to the opponent gaining a passed pawn. Let's show a couple of puzzle examples of this next. } ) 

