 { Find the best move for White. } 1. { -KEY- } Rc5 $1 { Correct! Using the outpost is the only way for White to apply pressure to the opponent, anything else would lead to pretty dry equality. } Ne8 { Black suddenly has to be pretty precise. Their idea here is to bring the knight to d6 in order to defend the c8 rook. } ( { The problem for Black is that they can never take on c5 in good circumstances: } 1... Rxc5 $2 2. dxc5 $16 { White will play b3-b4 next, locking in a powerful protected passed pawn. } ) 
2. Nf3 Nd6 3. Ne5 Qd8 4. Qc3 Kg7 5. Rxc8 Qxc8 6. Qc5 $1 { Again the outpost causes Black a headache, this time with the queen. } Qd8 7. Qa7 $16 { Black faces a really difficult defensive task over the coming moves, as all of White's pieces are much more active and the queenside pawns are at risk of falling. If you recall, in the 'Outposts' chapter we discussed a special kind of outpost which we termed a power outpost. These are outposts that are defended by two pawns, making them especially strong. We can also use these to our advantage when battling on an open file. Let's take a look at a couple of examples of this now. } 
 *

