 { In an online game against Magnus Carlsen, the well-known GM Daniel Naroditsky reached this position as Black. Can you spot the strong move that he played here? } 1... { -KEY- } Rd4 $1 { A strong move! Now White has to either leave the rook sit on a very active square, or change the structure. } ( { If Black had played a sloppy move like } 1... Kg7 { then after } 2. Rd5 $1 { White could have used his own outpost on d5 to build up the pressure along the d-file. } ) 
2. Rxd4 { White can even win a pawn by exchanging, but Black can bank on his piece activity to get it back. Here's how best play from both sides would look: } cxd4 3. Qxc7 Rxc7 4. Nd5 { The fork stops Black from capturing on c2. } Rc6 5. Nxb4 { White nets a pawn, but has shaky coordination. } Rc4 6. Nd5 Kg7 { Defending the bishop. } 7. c3 dxc3 8. Nxc3 Be7 { Activating the bishop. } 9. Kf1 Bb4 10. Rc1 Bxc3 11. Rxc3 Rxa4 $11 { Black's piece activity proved enough to recover the pawn. The endgame is completely equal. This example is one to watch out for, as the pattern of planting a rook on either d4 or d5 pops up quite a lot. } 
