 { In our final position of the section, we'll show an important pattern that pops up quite often where one side can combine power outposts with an exchange sacrifice. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 $1 { First, White secures a power outpost on c6. } Rc8 2. Rc6 $3 { A powerful exchange sacrifice. If Black ignores the rook then all of their pieces will be hemmed in. } ( { A timid move like } 2. Rxc8 { would leave Black in a winning position after } Qxc8 3. Kh2 Qc4 $19 { White cannot defend the pawn on b5, and to make matters worse Black has a powerful bishop pair. } ) 
Bxc6 3. bxc6+ { The protected passed pawn more than makes up for the lost material. } Ke8 4. h4 Rc7 5. f5 Qc8 6. h5 { White continues to hammer away at the kingside with a series of pawn breaks. } Bf6 ( 6... gxf5 $2 { The problem for Black is that if they capture then } 7. Qg5 { wins on the spot, since there is no way to stop Qg8+. } ) 
7. Qg3 Be5 8. Nf4 $18 { Black's pieces remain paralysed, while White is about to break through on g6. The position is lost. In fact, Black has nothing better than to play 8...Rxc6 and play down a full piece. This brings us to the end of the section. Whenever battling for open files, it pays to keep an eye out for outposts available to you or to your opponent. Power outposts are especially valuable, so it's worth considering any moves that allow you to establish one as in the current example. In the final section of the chapter we'll speak about the close cousin of open files: the semi-open file. } 
